WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
(slow music)

2
00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:06.060
If the person who made it says it's art, then it's art.

3
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.280
So, it's all about the intention.

4
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.373 line:15%
Artists are people who make things for other people.

5
00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:17.400 line:15%
Art communicates things that everything else

6
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.699 line:15%
fails short to do.

7
00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:20.250 line:15%
(slow music continues)

8
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.794
Art is my North Star.

9
00:00:21.794 --> 00:00:25.044
(slow music continues)

10
00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:28.680
Today, we're gonna talk about art,

11
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.540
how to define it,

12
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:34.710
how to understand it, and why it matters.

13
00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.810
My conversational partner for this affair

14
00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.370
is Heidi Zuckerman,

15
00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:41.220
a global authority on contemporary art

16
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.610
and a woman I've known for over 30 years.

17
00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.520
Heidi is the former CEO and director

18
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.200
of the Aspen Art Museum,

19
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.570
which she successfully reimagined

20
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.610
as a world-class institution,

21
00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.680
and she's currently the CEO and director

22
00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.290
of the Orange County Museum of Art.

23
00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.950
Prior to both, she held posts at Berkeley Art Museum

24
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.100
and Pacific Film Archive and was a curator

25
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.800
of the Jewish Museum in New York.

26
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.290
In addition to curating literally hundreds

27
00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.300
of exhibitions over the years,

28
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.347
Heidi also hosts the podcast

29
00:01:14.347 --> 00:01:17.370
"Conversations About Art" and is the author

30
00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.160
of the "Conversations With Artists" book series.

31
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.650
This one's a little bit different from my usual affair,

32
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.420 line:15%
but very special, I think,

33
00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.820 line:15%
as Heidi is a very special person.

34
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.370 line:15%
I really enjoy talking to her, and I hope you dig it.

35
00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.751 line:15%
So, please hit that Subscribe button and enjoy.

36
00:01:32.751 --> 00:01:34.680
(soft music continues)

37
00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:37.410
I can't believe you're here.
(chuckles) Me neither.

38
00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:40.530 line:15%
The more I think about, like, the tapestry of our lives

39
00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:44.970 line:15%
and how they've overlapped at certain weird stages

40
00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.120 line:15%
over the years, like the more surreal it is.

41
00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.090
It's pretty wild to like sit across from you

42
00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.110
with all of this weird history.

43
00:01:54.349 --> 00:01:57.090
And I feel the same.

44
00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.460
You know, I just got back from Dallas and had

45
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.630
my YPO forum there.

46
00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.820
We spent the day with Rand Stagen,

47
00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.620
and I kind of track getting back in touch with you to him,

48
00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:14.103
and so many things in my life kind of come from him.

49
00:02:15.547 --> 00:02:16.710
So, yeah, I agree.

50
00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.953
Yeah, I was just in Miami and did a YPO event there.

51
00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.440
I'm sure.
I, like, hosted

52
00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.830
this whole, like, conference recently.

53
00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.171
We'll talk about the YPO stuff.

54
00:02:27.171 --> 00:02:29.280
Okay.
But I mean, going way back,

55
00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.530
just for people that are watching or listening,

56
00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.160
we met, I think it was '91, around that time,

57
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.113
maybe '92, something like that.

58
00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:40.440
I was dating your sister, Laura,

59
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.830
and I got quite close with your whole family.

60
00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.310
Like, I've been to your house in Huntington

61
00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.897
and, like-
Which is gone.

62
00:02:48.897 --> 00:02:50.550
I know, yeah, I know.
(Heidi laughing)

63
00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:52.170
I know Gary and your mom.

64
00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.730
How are they, are they good?

65
00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:56.040
Yeah, so we just got back

66
00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.290
from celebrating Gary's 70th birthday-

67
00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.123
Wow.
In London,

68
00:02:59.123 --> 00:03:02.430
and, well, you know, my mom

69
00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.130
had not gone on a plane across the ocean

70
00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:06.573
in a really long time.

71
00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.010
She wanted everyone to come to their lake house

72
00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:12.967
to celebrate his birthday, and I said,

73
00:03:12.967 --> 00:03:16.380
"I can't on March 24th, I'm gonna be in London,

74
00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.360
visiting Emerson," and I said, "you guys should come.

75
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.250
We should celebrate his birthday there,"

76
00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:23.520
and they picked up what I put down,

77
00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.700
and it kind of was a life-changing moment for them.

78
00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.290
So, I can tell you a little bit more about it,

79
00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:31.020
but it was great.
Yeah, it's so interesting.

80
00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.800
I mean, I have like a lot of very kind of vivid flashes

81
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.280
from that period of time, you know, little weird memories,

82
00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.970
but I think you were working at a gallery downtown,

83
00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.430
if memory serves me, around that era.

84
00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:45.263
Yeah, I think so.

85
00:03:45.263 --> 00:03:49.920
I mean, I started at the Jewish Museum in 1993.

86
00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.410
It was before that, yeah.
Yeah, so I had a couple

87
00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:56.580
of different jobs, like Stux Gallery and for Anne Levay.

88
00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.010
So, yeah, '91, '92.
Right.

89
00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:02.070
Yeah.
And Laura was doing acting,

90
00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:06.630
and I remember going to a site-specific theater performance

91
00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:08.313
that was in like an abandoned house.

92
00:04:08.313 --> 00:04:10.020
Like in the meat packing district?

93
00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:11.533
I think so, well, that was a different one.

94
00:04:11.533 --> 00:04:14.670
I remember that one, too.
Okay, that one was great.

95
00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.250
And she was working with Darren Aronofsky

96
00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.950
and had worked on "Pi" and "Requiem for a Dream".

97
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.000
Yes.

98
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.510
It was just a really cool time to be in New York.

99
00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.780
I feel like it was a moment where New York

100
00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.390
was still very dynamic, but you could feel

101
00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:34.020
that that was not gonna persist much longer.

102
00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.580
It was definitely edgy,

103
00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.010
and, you know, the intersection

104
00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:39.720
between what Laura was doing,

105
00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.290
which started out as theater 'cause she had been at NYU,

106
00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:46.530
but it was really veering into performance art,

107
00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:50.790
and it was kind of a time in the art world before people,

108
00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.310
I mean, of course, there was performance art in the '70s,

109
00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:57.150
but what she was doing really kind of predated

110
00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:01.593
a lot of the self-focused work that came after that.

111
00:05:02.534 --> 00:05:03.720
And now she's married

112
00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.430
and lives in Copenhagen?
Yes.

113
00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:06.917
Yeah, has kids and stuff like that.

114
00:05:06.917 --> 00:05:09.210
Is she good?
She's good, too.

115
00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.160
I'm glad to hear that.

116
00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:15.693
They were all in London, and we did this thing.

117
00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.060
I suggested it, and some people were more comfortable

118
00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.920
and other people were less comfortable,

119
00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:26.010
but the idea was to go around the table

120
00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:30.060
and everyone to say something that they love about Gary,

121
00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:33.630
and to use that language, and then, when we got to Gary,

122
00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:35.610
whose birthday it was, he had to say something

123
00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.740
that he loved about himself.

124
00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:41.760
And Mike, my brother, went first, and then Laura went,

125
00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.500
and I don't know if they used love.

126
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.840
I think they said, you know, like, one of the things I like,

127
00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.343
and then Laura's older son said,

128
00:05:48.343 --> 00:05:51.300
"You know, one of the things I love about Gary,"

129
00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.240
and it just kind of changed the whole tone,

130
00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.110
and it was very moving.
Yeah, that's sweet.

131
00:05:58.110 --> 00:05:58.943
That's cool.

132
00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:04.530
That phase of our lives was only the beginning

133
00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.330
because we've crisscrossed.

134
00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:08.160
We have lots of mutual friends in common

135
00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.400
that come from very distinct and dissimilar places.

136
00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:14.487
Just the other day I was with Rickey Gates.

137
00:06:14.487 --> 00:06:15.840
Oh, yeah.
He's in the middle

138
00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.270
of doing this thing that's called Ultra Training.

139
00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:20.310
Do you know about this?
I don't think so.

140
00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:22.740
So, this is his latest project,

141
00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.780
which is taking an Amtrak train.

142
00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:26.760
He left his home in Santa Fe,

143
00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:30.120
and he's just getting off at every stop and running around

144
00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.690
and doing this sort of a truncated run every street thing

145
00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:36.750
at every stop along the way, and he came through LA.

146
00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:39.240
So, I went down to Downtown Los Angeles.

147
00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:41.730
I have a back problem right now, so I can't really run,

148
00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:43.080
and I was like, "I'll come and meet you,

149
00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:46.020
but we're gonna have to walk," and we just ended up walking

150
00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:49.230
all around the Arts District and talking,

151
00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:52.020
and he's filming it for a documentary project.

152
00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.150
And, of course, your name came up

153
00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.760
because it's so bizarre that, like, you're friends

154
00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.920
with Rickey Gates because of the Aspen thing.

155
00:06:58.920 --> 00:06:59.753
Yeah.

156
00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:03.843
And, you know, how he presents himself,

157
00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:08.490
at the intersection of, like, art and athleticism.

158
00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:09.453
Yes.
Like, he is an artist.

159
00:07:09.453 --> 00:07:11.310
Yes.
And everything he does

160
00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:13.590
is some amalgam

161
00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.170
of performance art

162
00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:17.910
and kind of ephemera,

163
00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:19.950
like running around is something

164
00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:21.960
you can't really capture unless you're filming it,

165
00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:25.770
and that becomes these projects with photography and film

166
00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.840
and his writing and all of that.

167
00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.360
Like, he's a very unique guy in that regard,

168
00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.940
but we had a very fond conversation about you.

169
00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:37.100
Thank you, and did he tell you about the Hut Run Hut?

170
00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.820
He did, yes.
Yeah.

171
00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:39.990
So, explain that.

172
00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:41.730
The idea is that you're gonna curate

173
00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:44.610
one of his Hut Run Hut expeditions

174
00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:47.880
and have artists, like, in residence to kind of share

175
00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:49.950
at the end of the day of running around.

176
00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:51.990
Yeah, artists and athletes.
Yeah.

177
00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:56.070
And so, I'm really interested in this idea of a practice,

178
00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:59.100
and it comes from, you know, Buddhism, right,

179
00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:01.230
and this idea of like a life practice.

180
00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:04.740
And it started for me as like a curatorial practice,

181
00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.930
and then, you know, a life practice,

182
00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:09.660
and then this idea of, you know, who practices.

183
00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:12.750
So, artists practice and athletes practice,

184
00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:14.670
and of course, musicians practice, too,

185
00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.530
but you know, what does that mean?

186
00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:18.810
So, it's like you do something repeatedly,

187
00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.520
and then you can get better at it,

188
00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:22.890
but also, you know, there's variety to it.

189
00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:25.470
Some days you're stronger, some days you're tired,

190
00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:26.940
some days you, you know,

191
00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:29.220
are bursting with energy and ideas,

192
00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:32.130
and other days, like, you have nothing, right?

193
00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:35.100
And so, Rickey and I started talking about that

194
00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:37.967
and how it would be really interesting,

195
00:08:37.967 --> 00:08:41.190
'cause I'm also fascinated with this idea

196
00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:42.510
of kind of pushing the limits,

197
00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:44.190
which I know you are, too.
Right.

198
00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:46.800
So, what's that point of like stretching people

199
00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.830
to, like, being uncomfortable,

200
00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.350
but not, like, unbearably uncomfortable?

201
00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:55.983
And so, this endurance idea of, you know, running,

202
00:08:57.327 --> 00:09:00.090
or trail hiking, you know, probably,

203
00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:03.420
like, it doesn't have to be a run, you know, motion

204
00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:05.820
for five days and five nights,

205
00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:09.570
a hundred miles over five days at altitude,

206
00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:13.170
and to invite, I think it can be 12 people total,

207
00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.570
so, you know, six artists and six athletes,

208
00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.300
and have everyone have that shared experience.

209
00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:20.820
And at the end of the day, of course, coming together

210
00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:25.350
and talking about, you know, what it feels like to do that.

211
00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:27.183
So, I've sort of been training,

212
00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:29.460
and I asked him how to train,

213
00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:31.560
and he's notorious for, like, not training.

214
00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:32.467
Yeah. (laughs)
He's like,

215
00:09:32.467 --> 00:09:35.160
"Just like go for an hour each Sunday,

216
00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:36.933
and then add an hour," you know?

217
00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:39.450
When is this happening?

218
00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:42.330
So, we're gonna do it in,

219
00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:46.680
I think, August of '23, yeah.
Next year, yeah.

220
00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:49.380
We were going to do it this year, and then when I took

221
00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:51.210
the job at the Orange County Museum of Art,

222
00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:54.330
I'm not good at saying no.
Too much going on.

223
00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:56.520
And I said, "Look, even for me,

224
00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:59.760
to do this hundred mile thing

225
00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:01.380
a month before we open the museum

226
00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:02.820
is, like, not practical."
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

227
00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:05.430
So, he said, "That's fine, we'll push it to 2023,"

228
00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:06.960
but I'm sure you're on the list.

229
00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:07.793
Yeah, like, he's like,

230
00:10:07.793 --> 00:10:09.090
"Will you do it," and I was like, "Yeah."

231
00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:10.263
I didn't know when it was, but I was like,

232
00:10:10.263 --> 00:10:12.270
that sounds cool and unique, for sure.

233
00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:15.030
Are we gonna sleep in that crazy bus thing?

234
00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:16.740
No, we're gonna stay in these huts.

235
00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:18.930
So, there's like a hut system,

236
00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:21.870
like the 10th Mountain Division had these huts at altitude.

237
00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:23.470
But this is the original idea behind this.

238
00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:25.620
Yeah, yeah.
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

239
00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:26.730
Yeah, all right.

240
00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:27.990
Well, that'll be cool.
We're putting together

241
00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:29.310
the list, yeah.
Yeah, who else is,

242
00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:30.660
who, what other-
Lance said yes, too.

243
00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:32.310
Oh, cool.
Yeah.

244
00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:34.800
Who were the artists that you're thinking about for this?

245
00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.473
So, I have a couple of ideas,

246
00:10:39.052 --> 00:10:41.460
and, oh, I also asked Gretchen Bleiler,

247
00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:44.370
and she's actually the one that introduced me to Rickey,

248
00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:46.047
and so she's a yes, too.
Yeah, cool.

249
00:10:46.047 --> 00:10:47.910
So, the athletes are easier-

250
00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:49.800
She's a badass, yeah.
Than the artists, yeah.

251
00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:51.150
She's a total badass

252
00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.210
and a really, really close friend of mine.

253
00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:56.640
So, I don't have any of the artists committed yet,

254
