I could add to it, there was something for me to do.

760
00:33:33.210 --> 00:33:35.550
And it's on schedule for October,

761
00:33:35.550 --> 00:33:36.450
to open?
October 8th, 5:00 p.m.,

762
00:33:36.450 --> 00:33:39.360
24-hour opening.
Super exciting.

763
00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:41.610
I think there's like a $97 million budget,

764
00:33:41.610 --> 00:33:43.320
or something like that, that I read.

765
00:33:43.320 --> 00:33:45.037
This is no small project.
Yeah, hopefully it's 94,

766
00:33:45.037 --> 00:33:48.510
but it's- (laughing)
(laughing) And I know,

767
00:33:48.510 --> 00:33:52.020
I re-listened to the podcast that you did with Lance

768
00:33:52.020 --> 00:33:53.920
on Lance's show, and you were talking

769
00:33:56.062 --> 00:34:00.660
about keeping it on schedule, like it just had to open up

770
00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:02.280
that date, and everybody was gonna say,

771
00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:04.320
well, you know, it's inclement weather,

772
00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:05.910
like, we're gonna need a couple more months,

773
00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:08.070
and you were able to, like, stick the landing

774
00:34:08.070 --> 00:34:10.890
and open it as promised.

775
00:34:10.890 --> 00:34:11.723
That is true.

776
00:34:11.723 --> 00:34:13.620
That is true, yeah.
Yeah.

777
00:34:13.620 --> 00:34:17.460
And that was the first podcast I was ever on,

778
00:34:17.460 --> 00:34:20.460
and Lance is a, you know, great longtime friend,

779
00:34:20.460 --> 00:34:22.810
and he was a supporter of the Aspen Art Museum.

780
00:34:24.125 --> 00:34:27.243
And the story that you're referencing, you know, I sat down,

781
00:34:28.140 --> 00:34:29.550
the head of the building committee, you know,

782
00:34:29.550 --> 00:34:31.680
invited me to lunch, maybe in New York,

783
00:34:31.680 --> 00:34:35.040
and he, you know, said he was kind of trying to give me

784
00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:38.160
like a, you know, like, I don't know if you can say it,

785
00:34:38.160 --> 00:34:40.327
like come to Jesus, right, and he was like,

786
00:34:40.327 --> 00:34:42.817
"You know, this isn't gonna happen," and I was like,

787
00:34:42.817 --> 00:34:44.947
"Yes, this has to happen."
Right.

788
00:34:44.947 --> 00:34:47.010
You know, we kind of went back and forth, and he was like,

789
00:34:47.010 --> 00:34:48.604
"I don't think you understand," and I was like,

790
00:34:48.604 --> 00:34:52.284
"No, I don't think you understand."

791
00:34:52.284 --> 00:34:54.834
And he was great, you know, great to work with him,

792
00:34:56.037 --> 00:34:58.953
and yeah, a lot of things can get done through sheer will.

793
00:35:00.090 --> 00:35:02.340
So, this one will open on time as well.

794
00:35:02.340 --> 00:35:03.420
Yes, it will.
Yeah.

795
00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:06.900
Another interesting kind of weird thread

796
00:35:06.900 --> 00:35:10.860
is that Thom Mayne was the mentor to Lorcan O'Herlihy,

797
00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:12.570
who's the architect that we worked with

798
00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:13.530
on our house.
Yes.

799
00:35:13.530 --> 00:35:15.840
So, those threads.

800
00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:17.220
Yeah.
That layered even more.

801
00:35:17.220 --> 00:35:18.720
And Thom's amazing.

802
00:35:18.720 --> 00:35:20.431
He's got a great story.
He's a visionary.

803
00:35:20.431 --> 00:35:21.264
Yeah.

804
00:35:22.536 --> 00:35:26.310
You know, he's a son of, you know, Orange County, LA,

805
00:35:26.310 --> 00:35:28.470
you know, born in Indiana

806
00:35:28.470 --> 00:35:33.150
and moved out to California, raised by a single mom,

807
00:35:33.150 --> 00:35:36.000
and, you know, also, through sheer will,

808
00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:39.550
has built this incredible career, and I mean, genius.

809
00:35:39.550 --> 00:35:40.383
He's a genius.
Yeah, I mean,

810
00:35:40.383 --> 00:35:42.540
he's responsible for kind of creating

811
00:35:42.540 --> 00:35:46.710
the architectural iconography of Los Angeles in many ways,

812
00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:48.180
like it all tracks back to him,

813
00:35:48.180 --> 00:35:51.450
so that has to be a cool, enriching experience.

814
00:35:51.450 --> 00:35:54.090
Well, let's talk about art in general.

815
00:35:54.090 --> 00:35:57.390
I think art, it's like, how do we talk about art, right?

816
00:35:57.390 --> 00:35:59.820
Well, this is what you do for a living.

817
00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:02.610
Art means many things to many people.

818
00:36:02.610 --> 00:36:04.920
So, how do you think about what art is,

819
00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:07.710
like how do you define it for yourself?

820
00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:10.470
So, the way I define it

821
00:36:10.470 --> 00:36:14.670
for other people is also kind of how I define it for myself,

822
00:36:14.670 --> 00:36:16.500
but people often ask me that,

823
00:36:16.500 --> 00:36:19.350
and I have a super simple definition.

824
00:36:19.350 --> 00:36:23.220
What I say is that if the person who made it says it's art,

825
00:36:23.220 --> 00:36:24.720
then it's art.

826
00:36:24.720 --> 00:36:26.820
So, it's all about the intention.

827
00:36:26.820 --> 00:36:28.530
It's about the intention of the creator.

828
00:36:28.530 --> 00:36:31.770
And so, if you go through the galleries

829
00:36:31.770 --> 00:36:34.920
of The Metropolitan Museum of Art

830
00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:37.950
and you see like a beautiful Islamic bowl,

831
00:36:37.950 --> 00:36:40.833
you know, from the 9th century,

832
00:36:42.390 --> 00:36:43.223
I love that.

833
00:36:43.223 --> 00:36:45.660
You know, that's part of my aesthetic.

834
00:36:45.660 --> 00:36:47.760
You know, is that art, or is that artifact?

835
00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:50.490
Well, you know, probably it's artifact

836
00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:55.053
because it was used to prepare food, to present food, right?

837
00:36:56.220 --> 00:37:00.453
If it was created to be put up on a shelf to, you know,

838
00:37:03.803 --> 00:37:06.210
represent something that is not utilitarian,

839
00:37:06.210 --> 00:37:07.740
then it's an art object.

840
00:37:07.740 --> 00:37:10.290
So, if I take this and I move it over here,

841
00:37:10.290 --> 00:37:12.240
and I say, "Do you like my art, Rich,"

842
00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:14.340
and you're like, "Uh,"
(Rich laughing)

843
00:37:14.340 --> 00:37:16.717
you get to have an opinion, and you can say, like,

844
00:37:16.717 --> 00:37:18.450
"Yes, I think that's so amazing,

845
00:37:18.450 --> 00:37:20.340
the way the light hits the water," you know,

846
00:37:20.340 --> 00:37:24.030
or you can say, like, "No, because, you know,

847
00:37:24.030 --> 00:37:25.620
it's only three quarters full,

848
00:37:25.620 --> 00:37:28.320
and I would like it if it was, like, 100% full."

849
00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:30.450
Like, you get to have an opinion about it.

850
00:37:30.450 --> 00:37:31.530
You get to judge it,

851
00:37:31.530 --> 00:37:33.090
whether it's, like, good or bad,

852
00:37:33.090 --> 00:37:34.680
or you like it, or you don't,

853
00:37:34.680 --> 00:37:37.200
but no one gets to say that it's not art,

854
00:37:37.200 --> 00:37:39.780
you know, if I made it and I said it's art.

855
00:37:39.780 --> 00:37:41.700
Intentionality is all of it, then.

856
00:37:41.700 --> 00:37:44.220
So, anybody can say anything is art,

857
00:37:44.220 --> 00:37:48.300
and we're not, we're not able

858
00:37:48.300 --> 00:37:51.150
to say, "No, it's not."
Correct.

859
00:37:51.150 --> 00:37:52.950
That's a pretty bold definition.

860
00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:54.330
Yes.
That might make

861
00:37:54.330 --> 00:37:57.783
some people angry.
That's okay, you know.

862
00:37:58.920 --> 00:38:02.430
I did a donor trip to New York some years ago,

863
00:38:02.430 --> 00:38:05.910
and we went to the studio of an artist named Gedy Sibony,

864
00:38:05.910 --> 00:38:09.240
and he does lots of things, but at the time,

865
00:38:09.240 --> 00:38:13.290
he was taking carpet remnants and turning them around

866
00:38:13.290 --> 00:38:16.680
and hanging them on the wall, kind of like paintings, right?

867
00:38:16.680 --> 00:38:20.400
And a guy who was on the trip was so mad.

868
00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:22.200
He was like so offended by this

869
00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:24.030
'cause I was like, "This is so interesting,

870
00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:27.210
look at the tape mark on it and how it's been cut

871
00:38:27.210 --> 00:38:29.070
and, you know, how it comes off the wall,"

872
00:38:29.070 --> 00:38:32.070
and he was like, "Is this "The Emperor's New Clothes" thing?

873
00:38:32.951 --> 00:38:34.200
Are you trying to embarrass me?

874
00:38:34.200 --> 00:38:35.670
Are you trying to make me feel stupid?"

875
00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:37.350
Like, he was really angry,

876
00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:39.540
and he even insisted that we go out to dinner

877
00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:41.850
once we had gotten back to Aspen

878
00:38:41.850 --> 00:38:43.200
'cause he wanted to talk about it.

879
00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:45.720
It still bothered him like two weeks later,

880
00:38:45.720 --> 00:38:49.110
and I won't use his name, but I said, "You know what?

881
00:38:49.110 --> 00:38:51.810
Maybe the fact that you're still thinking about this

882
00:38:51.810 --> 00:38:54.900
and still angry about it means that it's actually good."

883
00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:57.180
Yeah, who wins in that situation?

884
00:38:57.180 --> 00:38:58.500
If it's haunting him,

885
00:38:58.500 --> 00:39:00.720
then that obviously is an argument

886
00:39:00.720 --> 00:39:04.110
that it does have some level of artistic merit.

887
00:39:04.110 --> 00:39:05.340
Yeah, or at least just worthy

888
00:39:05.340 --> 00:39:06.590
of being considered.
As a piece of, you know,

889
00:39:07.740 --> 00:39:12.300
being a provocateur, a provocative piece of something.

890
00:39:12.300 --> 00:39:16.500
Yeah, you know, I would much rather have someone,

891
00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:18.300
it's not like I want to make anyone mad,

892
00:39:18.300 --> 00:39:19.680
that's not what I'm saying,

893
00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:23.340
and I want people to care about stuff, you know?

894
00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:26.460
I think, like, lack of curiosity and apathy

895
00:39:26.460 --> 00:39:29.670
are what's gonna kill, not just individuals,

896
00:39:29.670 --> 00:39:31.050
but, like, society.

897
00:39:31.050 --> 00:39:33.060
So, I think it's great,

898
00:39:33.060 --> 00:39:34.410
you know, for people-
Well, let's move

899
00:39:34.410 --> 00:39:36.613
into that then, like why does art matter?

900
00:39:36.613 --> 00:39:37.953
(Heidi laughing)
Right?

901
00:39:39.300 --> 00:39:41.160
I'm sure you get asked these questions all the time.

902
00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:44.520
No, I'm just laughing because, you know, on my podcast,

903
00:39:44.520 --> 00:39:46.057
the one thing that I ask everyone is,

904
00:39:46.057 --> 00:39:47.490
"Why does art matter?"
Yeah, I know.

905
00:39:47.490 --> 00:39:49.230
And I figured you knew,

906
00:39:49.230 --> 00:39:52.330
and I've had this kind of personal campaign

907
00:39:54.030 --> 00:39:56.430
since I took the job in Aspen in 2005,

908
00:39:56.430 --> 00:40:00.360
where I wanted to kind of make the statement

909
00:40:00.360 --> 00:40:04.470
that by asking the question, "Why does art matter,"

910
00:40:04.470 --> 00:40:06.270
it's not, "Does art matter,"

911
00:40:06.270 --> 00:40:07.650
it's, "Why does it matter?"

912
00:40:07.650 --> 00:40:10.650
So, inherent in the question is, like,

913
00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:11.879
the assertion that it does.
You're assuming

914
00:40:11.879 --> 00:40:13.385
that it already does, yeah.
Exactly.

915
00:40:13.385 --> 00:40:15.750
So, that was intentional on my part,

916
00:40:15.750 --> 00:40:18.850
and, you know, I think that art matters

917
00:40:20.737 --> 00:40:24.840
because it is, it's like an alternate language.

918
00:40:24.840 --> 00:40:27.090
It's a way of communicating things

919
00:40:27.090 --> 00:40:30.450
that have no other way to be told.

920
00:40:30.450 --> 00:40:35.100
And earlier today, I had a meeting with a journalist,

921
00:40:35.100 --> 00:40:38.130
and she was saying that she had studied poetry,

922
00:40:38.130 --> 00:40:39.930
and she went to her kid's class

923
00:40:39.930 --> 00:40:42.360
and they were doing a poetry project,

924
00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:44.310
and they had a similar thing,

925
00:40:44.310 --> 00:40:47.310
like, you know, why does poetry matter, and she said,

926
00:40:47.310 --> 00:40:48.990
or maybe they said, like, "What is poetry,"

927
00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:53.940
and she said, "Poetry is extraordinary language,"

928
00:40:53.940 --> 00:40:56.040
and I was like, "That's amazing."

929
00:40:56.040 --> 00:40:58.950
Like, I love this idea of the extraordinary,

930
00:40:58.950 --> 00:41:00.270
and you know, she was saying, like,

931
00:41:00.270 --> 00:41:01.770
metaphor and simile matter,

932
00:41:01.770 --> 00:41:05.730
not 'cause we're trying to compare everything,

933
00:41:05.730 --> 00:41:09.420
but it's a way of giving us an ability to talk about things

934
00:41:09.420 --> 00:41:13.560
that we don't have an ability to talk about otherwise.

935
00:41:13.560 --> 00:41:16.320
So, art, for me, communicates things

936
00:41:16.320 --> 00:41:18.850
that everything else fails short

937
00:41:20.820 --> 00:41:21.653
to do.

938
00:41:22.710 --> 00:41:24.660
There's a difference between art

939
00:41:24.660 --> 00:41:27.210
in that kind of expanded concept

940
00:41:27.210 --> 00:41:31.350
versus the experience of art in the museum setting.

941
00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:32.580
Like when we think about art,

942
00:41:32.580 --> 00:41:34.470
we think about paintings hanging on a wall,

943
00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:37.050
or we think about a statue that's in the middle

944
00:41:37.050 --> 00:41:39.540
of a room in a museum, and when we think about museums,

945
00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:41.490
and I'm as guilty of this as anybody,

946
00:41:41.490 --> 00:41:45.390
and I'm certainly no savvy art person,

947
00:41:45.390 --> 00:41:48.210
but that experience

948
00:41:48.210 --> 00:41:50.100
conjures for me,

949
00:41:50.100 --> 00:41:53.220
like, names on a wall of donors

950
00:41:53.220 --> 00:41:56.970
who tend to be kind of robber baron, Vanderbilt-type people

951
00:41:56.970 --> 00:41:58.800
that you'll never meet,

952
00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:03.300
cryptic descriptions of the artists and the art

953
00:42:03.300 --> 00:42:07.200
that would confound even the most earnest person

954
00:42:07.200 --> 00:42:09.813
who's trying to understand what this is,

955
00:42:11.460 --> 00:42:13.470
and kind of an environment that can be cold

956
00:42:13.470 --> 00:42:16.680
and intimidating and somewhat off-putting.

957
00:42:16.680 --> 00:42:20.340
It's like the museum is about money,

958
00:42:20.340 --> 00:42:21.780
endowments,

959
00:42:21.780 --> 00:42:24.120
donors, prestige,

960
00:42:24.120 --> 00:42:26.553
power, and fancy parties.

961
00:42:27.570 --> 00:42:30.000
Yeah, that's-
So, disabuse me of this idea

962
00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:32.610
or help me understand how we can,

963
00:42:32.610 --> 00:42:36.186
'cause I know, like, at the center of everything that you do

964
00:42:36.186 --> 00:42:40.740
is this deep desire to democratize the art experience,

965
00:42:40.740 --> 00:42:44.910
to make it accessible and meaningful for everybody,

966
00:42:44.910 --> 00:42:48.480
because under your definition of what art is,

967
00:42:48.480 --> 00:42:50.460
it should be speaking to all of us.

968
00:42:50.460 --> 00:42:54.720
It has the power to elevate all of our life experience,

969
00:42:54.720 --> 00:42:59.053
but we need a better way to communicate about it

970
00:42:59.053 --> 00:43:02.010
and a better way to, like, access it.

971
00:43:02.010 --> 00:43:05.340
I agree with that last statement a lot,

972
00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:07.680
and I have spent

973
00:43:07.680 --> 00:43:10.470
a lot of time thinking about why, you know,

974
00:43:10.470 --> 00:43:13.830
why do a lot of people not care about art,

975
00:43:13.830 --> 00:43:15.720
because a lot of people don't care about art,

976
00:43:15.720 --> 00:43:18.787
and that was really, that was kind of my sabbatical project,

977
00:43:18.787 --> 00:43:22.350
you know, and that was part of the HiZ.art platform,

978
00:43:22.350 --> 00:43:25.710
is, you know, like what can I do personally

979
00:43:25.710 --> 00:43:28.380
to take art directly to the people,

980
00:43:28.380 --> 00:43:30.660
how can I mainline art,

981
00:43:30.660 --> 00:43:35.010
and I thought that I wanted to move away from a building

982
00:43:35.010 --> 00:43:37.530
that can be intimidating or associative

983
00:43:37.530 --> 00:43:39.450
with exactly what you're talking about.

984
00:43:39.450 --> 00:43:44.450
So, you know, is there a more direct vehicle to communicate

985
00:43:44.670 --> 00:43:46.970
with the broadest possible audience about art?

986
00:43:49.138 --> 00:43:52.650
I have been listening to the recent Brené Brown book,

987
00:43:52.650 --> 00:43:57.120
and one thing that was in there that really stood out for me

988
00:43:57.120 --> 00:44:00.450
was about this idea, and you know, you may have read it

989
00:44:00.450 --> 00:44:02.070
or, you know, heard it as well,

990
00:44:02.070 --> 00:44:06.090
which is that, it was about curiosity and brain function

991
00:44:06.090 --> 00:44:08.970
and how we literally cannot be curious about something

992
00:44:08.970 --> 00:44:10.440
that we know nothing about.

993
00:44:10.440 --> 00:44:14.400
You have to have some seed from which to be curious,

994
00:44:14.400 --> 00:44:17.490
and so if people have no knowledge of art

995
00:44:17.490 --> 00:44:19.290
or no access point for art,

996
00:44:19.290 --> 00:44:21.660
then, of course, they're not gonna be curious about it,

997
00:44:21.660 --> 00:44:23.100
and people don't care about things

998
00:44:23.100 --> 00:44:24.690
that they're not curious about.

999
00:44:24.690 --> 00:44:28.470
So, how do you get over that hurdle,

1000
00:44:28.470 --> 00:44:30.720
those associations of contemporary art

1001
00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:33.870
with, you know, an Andy Warhol silkscreen

1002
00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:37.680
that just sold for $195 million to Larry Gagosian, right,

1003
00:44:37.680 --> 00:44:42.450
like all these things that work against this notion of art

1004
00:44:42.450 --> 00:44:47.450
being this system of energy, this means of communication?

1005
00:44:48.030 --> 00:44:50.310
So, you know, we make some progress,

1006
00:44:50.310 --> 00:44:52.110
and then something like that happens, right,

1007
00:44:52.110 --> 00:44:54.930
so there's this kind of push/pull, you know, within that.

1008
00:44:54.930 --> 00:44:58.320
So, for me, part of what I'm trying to do

1009
00:44:58.320 --> 00:45:01.290
with the Orange County Museum of Art is to build

1010
00:45:01.290 --> 00:45:04.590
what I'm referring to as a museum of the 21st century,

1011
00:45:04.590 --> 00:45:06.510
and part of the way that we're doing that

1012
00:45:06.510 --> 00:45:11.130
is through an exhibition program that has three tenets.

1013
00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:14.100
One of the tenets is looking back to look forward,

1014
00:45:14.100 --> 00:45:15.340
and so this idea

1015
00:45:17.228 --> 00:45:19.080
of not thinking like you're the first person

1016
00:45:19.080 --> 00:45:20.970
that's ever had this idea, right,

1017
00:45:20.970 --> 00:45:24.040
or not thinking like, you know, it's only about us,

1018
00:45:24.040 --> 00:45:26.580
well, you know, right now, and what can we consume,

1019
00:45:26.580 --> 00:45:29.970
and what do we need, and how are we satiated, you know?

1020
00:45:29.970 --> 00:45:34.110
And, you know, the next tenet is about place

1021
00:45:34.110 --> 00:45:36.810
and being really cognizant that, you know,

1022
00:45:36.810 --> 00:45:39.390
particularly during the COVID period, and after,

1023
00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:41.970
like, you know, as I said, like the music stopped,

1024
00:45:41.970 --> 00:45:44.730
and you were, you know, you were where you were,

1025
00:45:44.730 --> 00:45:46.680
and so, like, you know, what did you learn

1026
00:45:46.680 --> 00:45:50.070
about, like, yourself absent of motion?

1027
00:45:50.070 --> 00:45:52.170
You know, so many people spend, myself included,

1028
00:45:52.170 --> 00:45:54.847
so much time in motion, you know, that there was,

1029
00:45:54.847 --> 00:45:58.800
you know, a time that we wouldn't maybe otherwise have had

1030
00:45:58.800 --> 00:46:00.540
to say, like, "Well, who am I,"

1031
00:46:00.540 --> 00:46:02.160
not just, "Where am I?"

1032
00:46:02.160 --> 00:46:05.580
And then the third tenet we're calling caring and sharing,

1033
00:46:05.580 --> 00:46:08.850
you know, and it's about this idea we want the museum to be,

1034
00:46:08.850 --> 00:46:12.300
like, you know, a Netflix series or great restaurant

1035
00:46:12.300 --> 00:46:14.820
or, you know, something that you say to your friend,

1036
00:46:14.820 --> 00:46:16.920
like, "Hey, you have to check this out,"

1037
00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:19.710
like, "Let's go and do that together, I'll take you there,"

1038
00:46:19.710 --> 00:46:23.190
and so to not have the museum be like I'm telling you

1039
00:46:23.190 --> 00:46:25.470
what's great and what you should care about

1040
00:46:25.470 --> 00:46:27.360
because I'm some authority figure.

1041
00:46:27.360 --> 00:46:29.940
Right, or it feels like homework.

1042
00:46:29.940 --> 00:46:31.230
Yes, exactly.
As opposed to something

1043
00:46:31.230 --> 00:46:33.363
you're excited to check out.
Exactly.

1044
00:46:35.490 --> 00:46:38.370
It's one thing to say that or to set out to achieve that,

1045
00:46:38.370 --> 00:46:40.960
but what is the mechanism to hook people in?

1046
00:46:40.960 --> 00:46:43.570
I mean, I know one big thing is you have

1047
00:46:44.990 --> 00:46:48.600
a donor that's allowing you to make the museum free

1048
00:46:48.600 --> 00:46:51.240
for the first 10 years, so you've removed that hurdle,

1049
00:46:51.240 --> 00:46:52.980
which I think is huge.
Yeah.

1050
00:46:52.980 --> 00:46:57.120
But how do you get people talking about it and excited?

1051
00:46:57.120 --> 00:46:57.953
Yeah.

1052
00:46:59.190 --> 00:47:03.240
So, in Aspen, we did a project called Arte en Español,

1053
00:47:03.240 --> 00:47:06.030
and, you know, we really wanted to reach out

1054
00:47:06.030 --> 00:47:07.560
to the Spanish-speaking community,

1055
00:47:07.560 --> 00:47:09.840
and, you know, a lot of times,

1056
00:47:09.840 --> 00:47:11.850
institutions would say, like, "Hey, you know,

1057
00:47:11.850 --> 00:47:13.140
we have an artist from Mexico,"

1058
00:47:13.140 --> 00:47:15.180
like, "you guys should come,"

1059
00:47:15.180 --> 00:47:17.880
and it's like, "Well, you know, why," right?

1060
00:47:17.880 --> 00:47:20.730
So, we developed this partnership

1061
00:47:20.730 --> 00:47:23.910
with a Spanish language radio station called La Tricolor,

1062
00:47:23.910 --> 00:47:26.550
and at the time, the director of learning

1063
00:47:26.550 --> 00:47:28.380
and myself, you know, spoke Spanish,

1064
00:47:28.380 --> 00:47:31.200
and, you know, went on the station

1065
00:47:31.200 --> 00:47:33.120
and invited people to come.

1066
00:47:33.120 --> 00:47:35.160
And so, the first time, we thought, you know,

1067
00:47:35.160 --> 00:47:37.827
maybe we'd get 15 people, and we got 50, you know,

1068
00:47:37.827 --> 00:47:39.720
and the second time, we thought maybe we'd get 15,

1069
00:47:39.720 --> 00:47:41.160
and we got like 300.

1070
00:47:41.160 --> 00:47:44.370
And so, the learning from that

1071
00:47:44.370 --> 00:47:47.490
was something that we'll put in play here, too,

1072
00:47:47.490 --> 00:47:50.043
which is people want to be invited, you know?

1073
00:47:52.110 --> 00:47:54.000
Like, no one wants assumptions

1074
00:47:54.000 --> 00:47:56.940
to be made about them or their values

1075
00:47:56.940 --> 00:47:59.610
or, you know, what they should care about, right?

1076
00:47:59.610 --> 00:48:01.800
People want to be seen, people want to be heard,

1077
00:48:01.800 --> 00:48:06.000
people want to feel like you want them specifically.

1078
00:48:06.000 --> 00:48:07.980
You know, it's like you get an invitation

1079
00:48:07.980 --> 00:48:09.013
that's, like, to a whole bunch of people,

1080
00:48:09.013 --> 00:48:12.570
and you're like, yeah, I mean, maybe I'll go,

1081
00:48:12.570 --> 00:48:14.280
but if you get like a handwritten note,

1082
00:48:14.280 --> 00:48:17.130
or someone comes by your house, you know, or your office,

1083
00:48:17.130 --> 00:48:19.080
and they're like, "Hey, I'm doing this thing,

1084
00:48:19.080 --> 00:48:21.480
and I'd really like you to be there,"

1085
00:48:21.480 --> 00:48:22.620
it makes a huge difference.

1086
00:48:22.620 --> 00:48:25.290
So, I think that's part of what we're doing,

1087
00:48:25.290 --> 00:48:27.340
is, you know, working really hard

1088
00:48:28.567 --> 00:48:32.783
to be aware of the, like, unassumed biases

1089
00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:36.240
or assumed biases, or, you know,

1090
00:48:36.240 --> 00:48:39.960
to really be open and courageous about having conversations

1091
00:48:39.960 --> 00:48:41.977
that are uncomfortable, and to say, like,

1092
00:48:41.977 --> 00:48:44.223
"Okay, maybe it was this way before.

1093
00:48:45.540 --> 00:48:47.250
I'm sorry."

1094
00:48:47.250 --> 00:48:48.900
You know, like, "What else can I do?"

1095
00:48:48.900 --> 00:48:49.740
Yeah.

1096
00:48:49.740 --> 00:48:51.360
One of the things that I thought was really cool

1097
00:48:51.360 --> 00:48:52.780
about what you did in Aspen

1098
00:48:54.000 --> 00:48:56.790
was the outreach to the community.

1099
00:48:56.790 --> 00:48:58.590
I mean, Aspen's a very interesting place.

1100
00:48:58.590 --> 00:49:00.453
We think of it as being this,

1101
00:49:01.770 --> 00:49:04.770
you know, ski resort town that's very hoity-toity

1102
00:49:04.770 --> 00:49:06.960
and movie stars, et cetera,

1103
00:49:06.960 --> 00:49:10.860
but that's not the legacy or the history of that place.

1104
00:49:10.860 --> 00:49:12.300
That's a sort of recent development.

1105
00:49:12.300 --> 00:49:15.600
I mean, there was this counterculture hippie situation,

1106
00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:17.730
and then the vast history that preceded that.

1107
00:49:17.730 --> 00:49:21.030
Rickey was filling me in on a bunch of this the other day,

1108
00:49:21.030 --> 00:49:25.080
but the fact that the museum, through your leadership,

1109
00:49:25.080 --> 00:49:28.920
was connected to, like, a nearby rehab facility,

1110
00:49:28.920 --> 00:49:30.270
and the work that you did with, like,

1111
00:49:30.270 --> 00:49:31.770
the veterans community,

1112
00:49:31.770 --> 00:49:33.930
to bring them into that art experience

1113
00:49:33.930 --> 00:49:36.810
as a healing modality.
Yeah.

1114
00:49:36.810 --> 00:49:39.730
That's super important to me, you know, and I think

1115
00:49:40.675 --> 00:49:43.350
that sort of the highest order

1116
00:49:43.350 --> 00:49:45.720
of living is to be of service,

1117
00:49:45.720 --> 00:49:48.873
and so, you know, we hear about, like, servant leaders,

1118
00:49:50.160 --> 00:49:52.530
and I really put that at the forefront

1119
00:49:52.530 --> 00:49:54.240
of what I do every day,

1120
00:49:54.240 --> 00:49:56.340
is, you know, like how can I help?

1121
00:49:56.340 --> 00:49:59.580
And so, I'm an ideas generator, I'm like a visionary, right,

1122
00:49:59.580 --> 00:50:01.657
but I also am great-
Since Doug Aitken said,

1123
00:50:01.657 --> 00:50:03.140
"What else," you're like,

1124
00:50:03.140 --> 00:50:06.480
"I need to be better about my idea generation."

1125
00:50:06.480 --> 00:50:07.410
Quicker, yeah.
Yeah.

1126
00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:08.710
I got way quicker at it,

1127
00:50:09.870 --> 00:50:11.910
but you know, it's one thing to have an idea,

1128
00:50:11.910 --> 00:50:13.410
it's another thing to execute.

1129
00:50:14.730 --> 00:50:19.727
So, I've spent a lot of time thinking about, you know,

1130
00:50:19.727 --> 00:50:22.020
how can we help, like what can we do, you know,

1131
00:50:22.020 --> 00:50:24.120
and a story that I love to tell

1132
00:50:24.120 --> 00:50:26.640
is a collaboration that we did in Aspen,

1133
00:50:26.640 --> 00:50:29.010
and I'm talking kind of about that now

1134
00:50:29.010 --> 00:50:31.980
because the Orange County Museum of Art isn't open yet.

1135
00:50:31.980 --> 00:50:35.640
So, like you said, I can say things, but we have to do 'em,

1136
00:50:35.640 --> 00:50:37.990
but these are things, you know, that were done.

1137
00:50:39.540 --> 00:50:42.450
I reached out to the Hope Center,

1138
00:50:42.450 --> 00:50:45.450
which is a suicide prevention center in Aspen,

1139
00:50:45.450 --> 00:50:49.140
and I've lost some really important people

1140
00:50:49.140 --> 00:50:52.650
in my life to suicide, and that's one of the causes

1141
00:50:52.650 --> 00:50:54.300
that I, you know, work on behalf of,

1142
00:50:54.300 --> 00:50:56.850
and I said, "You know, I'd like to help.

1143
00:50:56.850 --> 00:50:59.460
Can you come to the museum and have a meeting,

1144
00:50:59.460 --> 00:51:01.227
and let's, you know, brainstorm."

1145
00:51:02.929 --> 00:51:05.070
So, you know, I met with the founder and the director,

1146
00:51:05.070 --> 00:51:07.860
and at first, they said, "Well, you know,

1147
00:51:07.860 --> 00:51:09.637
can you raise money for us," and I was like,

1148
00:51:09.637 --> 00:51:11.430
"No, I can't raise money for you,"

1149
00:51:11.430 --> 00:51:13.860
like maybe personally, but not as an institution.

1150
00:51:13.860 --> 00:51:15.960
And then they said, "All right, well, can you show art

1151
00:51:15.960 --> 00:51:17.790
that's made by, you know, our clients,"

1152
00:51:17.790 --> 00:51:19.650
and I was like, "No, you know, we can't do that either,

1153
00:51:19.650 --> 00:51:21.000
but tell me what you do."

1154
00:51:21.000 --> 00:51:22.920
So, you know, people call there

1155
00:51:22.920 --> 00:51:27.180
if they are in immediate danger

1156
00:51:27.180 --> 00:51:30.840
of personal harm than 911, hospital, et cetera,

1157
00:51:30.840 --> 00:51:32.310
and if people can be, like,

1158
00:51:32.310 --> 00:51:34.560
moved to a different place psychologically,

1159
00:51:34.560 --> 00:51:37.230
then they're given a list of safe spaces to go,

1160
00:51:37.230 --> 00:51:39.600
and I said, "Great, can we make the museum a safe space?"

1161
00:51:39.600 --> 00:51:44.130
So, we did frontline training for all of our employees

1162
00:51:44.130 --> 00:51:46.140
so that the museum could be a safe space,

1163
00:51:46.140 --> 00:51:48.690
and the idea is that, you know, sometimes all it takes

1164
00:51:48.690 --> 00:51:51.120
is like one person being kind to you

1165
00:51:51.120 --> 00:51:53.910
to move you from, like, this moment to this moment,

1166
00:51:53.910 --> 00:51:57.390
and it's in between those moments that you're at risk.

1167
00:51:57.390 --> 00:52:01.080
So, I can't remember what the question was,

1168
00:52:01.080 --> 00:52:01.913
but-
Yeah, well,

1169
00:52:01.913 --> 00:52:05.550
it was really about how you leverage your position

1170
00:52:05.550 --> 00:52:09.100
and your love of art and your belief in its ability

1171
00:52:10.265 --> 00:52:12.310
to be this vehicle for healing

1172
00:52:13.705 --> 00:52:16.650
or community or, you know,

1173
00:52:16.650 --> 00:52:19.803
anything, really, that is aspirational, right,

1174
00:52:21.073 --> 00:52:23.730
and how you channel that into service.

1175
00:52:23.730 --> 00:52:25.530
Like, okay, I'm in this privileged position,

1176
00:52:25.530 --> 00:52:27.330
I have this job, this role,

1177
00:52:27.330 --> 00:52:29.850
but I have the opportunity to be creative within that,

1178
00:52:29.850 --> 00:52:32.730
and how can I give back to the community

1179
00:52:32.730 --> 00:52:36.270
and instill in them some version of this passion

1180
00:52:36.270 --> 00:52:39.000
that I have that is so meaningful to me personally?

1181
00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:41.370
Yeah, and, you know, museums,

1182
00:52:41.370 --> 00:52:43.170
you know, so I'm back in the museum space,

1183
00:52:43.170 --> 00:52:45.090
I'm back in a building,

1184
00:52:45.090 --> 00:52:48.963
and there are certain constraints around that, you know,

1185
00:52:50.651 --> 00:52:54.000
but in other ways, it's also incredibly expansive.

1186
00:52:54.000 --> 00:52:55.170
You know, it's associative,

1187
00:52:55.170 --> 00:52:56.910
people know that's a place for art.

1188
00:52:56.910 --> 00:53:00.120
Museums are places that are often quiet, you know?

1189
00:53:00.120 --> 00:53:02.190
Yeah.
And so, it's great

1190
00:53:02.190 --> 00:53:04.500
for people with autism, you know,

1191
00:53:04.500 --> 00:53:07.740
like it's reduced stimulation of, you know, sound and light

1192
00:53:07.740 --> 00:53:09.990
'cause everything's super controlled, right?

1193
00:53:09.990 --> 00:53:12.840
So, there are things that we're in a really great position

1194
00:53:12.840 --> 00:53:14.373
to be of help with.

1195
00:53:15.540 --> 00:53:17.040
When you think about education

1196
00:53:17.040 --> 00:53:19.350
and the way that kids are taught art,

1197
00:53:19.350 --> 00:53:21.753
like, what are your thoughts on that?

1198
00:53:23.760 --> 00:53:24.990
You know, we learn from things

1199
00:53:24.990 --> 00:53:26.490
that are positive and negative, right?

1200
00:53:26.490 --> 00:53:30.840
So, I remember being at the Rothko Chapel in Houston once,

1201
00:53:30.840 --> 00:53:33.030
and that's one of my, like, favorite places,

1202
00:53:33.030 --> 00:53:35.190
like it's a go-to place for me,

1203
00:53:35.190 --> 00:53:39.150
and there was a group of kids, they were coming from school.

1204
00:53:39.150 --> 00:53:41.550
They were, you know, dressed in their school uniforms,

1205
00:53:41.550 --> 00:53:44.047
and their teacher said,

1206
00:53:44.047 --> 00:53:49.047
"Okay, go in, keep moving, don't touch anything, don't stop.

1207
00:53:49.050 --> 00:53:50.970
Walk to the end, take a left.

1208
00:53:50.970 --> 00:53:53.790
Walk to the end, take a left, come back out,"

1209
00:53:53.790 --> 00:53:55.500
and I was like, "Oh, my God."

1210
00:53:55.500 --> 00:53:58.800
Like, this is this incredible place of contemplation

1211
00:53:58.800 --> 00:54:01.830
and quiet and solitude and, like, you know,

1212
00:54:01.830 --> 00:54:04.800
magnitude and humanity, and here they're being told,

1213
00:54:04.800 --> 00:54:06.810
like, "You don't belong," right?

1214
00:54:06.810 --> 00:54:08.640
Like, "Don't do all these things,

1215
00:54:08.640 --> 00:54:11.100
I'm gonna assume that you're gonna do everything negative,"

1216
00:54:11.100 --> 00:54:13.627
instead of like, "Hey, go inside," you know,

1217
00:54:13.627 --> 00:54:17.040
"tell me which one is your favorite when you come out."

1218
00:54:17.040 --> 00:54:19.140
Like, "What do you think of," you know?

1219
00:54:19.140 --> 00:54:20.760
Like, "Keep your voice quiet

1220
00:54:20.760 --> 00:54:22.350
so other people can have contemplation."

1221
00:54:22.350 --> 00:54:25.800
There are ways to say the same thing, you know, differently.

1222
00:54:25.800 --> 00:54:30.800
So, I don't know what kids are being taught about art now.

1223
00:54:31.140 --> 00:54:33.900
You know, my kids went to Aspen Country Day School at first,

1224
00:54:33.900 --> 00:54:37.800
and, you know, they're three years apart in terms of grades,

1225
00:54:37.800 --> 00:54:40.260
and they had the same assigned (laughing) art project.

1226
00:54:40.260 --> 00:54:42.060
Like, it didn't evolve.
It didn't evolve

1227
00:54:42.060 --> 00:54:43.188
at all.
No, and it didn't evolve

1228
00:54:43.188 --> 00:54:45.152
any kind of creativity-
Like draw within the lines,

1229
00:54:45.152 --> 00:54:48.480
and if you do anything creative, that's no bueno.

1230
00:54:48.480 --> 00:54:50.640
Like, it's really a creativity killer,

1231
00:54:50.640 --> 00:54:53.370
which is the opposite of the whole intention behind it.

1232
00:54:53.370 --> 00:54:55.980
For sure, and I remember one of my kids actually got

1233
00:54:55.980 --> 00:54:58.590
in trouble because they didn't follow the assignment,

1234
00:54:58.590 --> 00:55:00.360
and I was like, but art is not

1235
00:55:00.360 --> 00:55:02.080
about an assignment, what do you mean?

1236
00:55:02.080 --> 00:55:03.660
Like, I would think you would get an A

1237
00:55:03.660 --> 00:55:05.410
if you don't follow the assignment.

1238
00:55:06.383 --> 00:55:07.590
(upbeat synth music)

1239
00:55:07.590 --> 00:55:10.200
Prophets walk among us.

1240
00:55:10.200 --> 00:55:13.800
As a writer and podcaster for nearly 10 years,

1241
00:55:13.800 --> 00:55:16.620
I've become more convinced than ever that our world

1242
00:55:16.620 --> 00:55:20.700
is populated by scores of beautiful and brilliant people

1243
00:55:20.700 --> 00:55:23.040
who have amazing stories to share,

1244
00:55:23.040 --> 00:55:24.357
those that we don't know,

1245
00:55:24.357 --> 00:55:26.280
who can teach us something new

1246
00:55:26.280 --> 00:55:27.480
and leave us all the better

1247
00:55:27.480 --> 00:55:30.240
for the experience of their sharing.

1248
00:55:30.240 --> 00:55:32.580
And so, I've dedicated my career to tracking down

1249
00:55:32.580 --> 00:55:35.010
the most compelling prophets on the planet,

1250
00:55:35.010 --> 00:55:38.100
going deep with each of them on my podcast

1251
00:55:38.100 --> 00:55:40.920
to elucidate the best of what they have to offer

1252
00:55:40.920 --> 00:55:45.330
and to sharing the insights gleaned for the benefit of all.

1253
00:55:45.330 --> 00:55:47.340
But the podcast is not the only medium

1254
00:55:47.340 --> 00:55:49.530
by which to share their stories,

1255
00:55:49.530 --> 00:55:51.840
which is why I'm proud to announce the release

1256
00:55:51.840 --> 00:55:55.650
of my new book, "Voicing Change, Volume Two".

1257
00:55:55.650 --> 00:55:57.817
More than mere words on paper,

1258
00:55:57.817 --> 00:56:01.860
"Voicing Change" is a physical manifestation of the magic,

1259
00:56:01.860 --> 00:56:06.540
inspiration, and timeless wisdom that transpires each week

1260
00:56:06.540 --> 00:56:08.820
on the Rich Roll podcast.

1261
00:56:08.820 --> 00:56:10.590
The first edition of "Voicing Change"

1262
00:56:10.590 --> 00:56:12.090
was a beautifully rendered book,

1263
00:56:12.090 --> 00:56:15.450
worthy of display on any coffee table,

1264
00:56:15.450 --> 00:56:18.150
and Volume Two follows in that tradition

1265
00:56:18.150 --> 00:56:21.750
by showcasing even more of my favorite conversations

1266
00:56:21.750 --> 00:56:23.790
in an elegant publication,

1267
00:56:23.790 --> 00:56:26.130
replete with interview excerpts,

1268
00:56:26.130 --> 00:56:28.860
essays, and stunning photography,

1269
00:56:28.860 --> 00:56:32.610
making for an exquisite companion to the first volume

1270
00:56:32.610 --> 00:56:34.983
or a satisfying standalone work.

1271
00:56:35.940 --> 00:56:39.120
Picking up this book allows you to revisit the wisdom

1272
00:56:39.120 --> 00:56:43.080
of your favorite everyday prophets and physically interact

1273
00:56:43.080 --> 00:56:45.783
with the life-changing ideas contained within.

1274
00:56:47.137 --> 00:56:49.140
"Voicing Change, Volume Two",

1275
00:56:49.140 --> 00:56:52.740
available now while supplies last for a limited time.

1276
00:56:52.740 --> 00:56:54.510
Order your copy today

1277
00:56:54.510 --> 00:56:56.883
only at richroll.com.

1278
00:56:58.680 --> 00:56:59.680
How do you think

1279
00:57:00.850 --> 00:57:05.640
about the role of art in this area of offense?

1280
00:57:05.640 --> 00:57:09.210
Like, we're very hypersensitized right now,

1281
00:57:09.210 --> 00:57:10.470
and we're going through, you know,

1282
00:57:10.470 --> 00:57:15.470
a lot of cultural shifts at the moment, for better or worse.

1283
00:57:15.510 --> 00:57:16.740
Like, you know, this is part

1284
00:57:16.740 --> 00:57:18.933
of what moves us forward as a culture,

1285
00:57:19.770 --> 00:57:23.160
but there does seem to be trepidation

1286
00:57:23.160 --> 00:57:26.130
around saying the wrong thing,

1287
00:57:26.130 --> 00:57:27.780
and how does that fit with art,

1288
00:57:27.780 --> 00:57:31.620
which is about being provocative or pushing the envelope

1289
00:57:31.620 --> 00:57:33.870
or making people uncomfortable?

1290
00:57:33.870 --> 00:57:38.040
Yeah, you know, I like to give everyone the benefit

1291
00:57:38.040 --> 00:57:39.750
of the doubt, you know,

1292
00:57:39.750 --> 00:57:43.200
and that's how I like to just approach my life, you know?

1293
00:57:43.200 --> 00:57:45.810
And, like, yeah, sometimes people

1294
00:57:45.810 --> 00:57:48.870
say things that are triggering

1295
00:57:48.870 --> 00:57:52.830
or offensive, you know, and I like to believe

1296
00:57:52.830 --> 00:57:55.710
that, you know, everyone does the best they can

1297
00:57:55.710 --> 00:57:58.110
with the information that they have at the time.

1298
00:57:59.490 --> 00:58:02.400
And, sure, like, I'm gonna say things wrong sometimes,

1299
00:58:02.400 --> 00:58:04.710
and other people are gonna say the wrong thing sometimes,

1300
00:58:04.710 --> 00:58:07.890
and, you know, to come at things with, again,

1301
00:58:07.890 --> 00:58:10.197
this kind of grace for people, you know,

1302
00:58:10.197 --> 00:58:14.790
and a belief in, like, the inherent, you know, goodness,

1303
00:58:14.790 --> 00:58:17.940
I think, is how, you know, I try and approach art, too.

1304
00:58:17.940 --> 00:58:20.940
And I think, you know, I've been in situations

1305
00:58:20.940 --> 00:58:22.200
where I'll say like, you know,

1306
00:58:22.200 --> 00:58:25.173
I'm not really sure if I can say this or, you know,

1307
00:58:26.220 --> 00:58:30.693
like, is this okay, and that kind of humility, you know,

1308
00:58:33.090 --> 00:58:36.000
to be open in that way, you know,

1309
00:58:36.000 --> 00:58:38.460
with that kind of grace and dignity

1310
00:58:38.460 --> 00:58:42.600
for whomever you're talking to or about,

1311
00:58:42.600 --> 00:58:45.873
I think that's the only answer I can give.

1312
00:58:46.890 --> 00:58:51.150
And what about the role of the artist themselves, though,

1313
00:58:51.150 --> 00:58:53.430
'cause they play a role in how we think

1314
00:58:53.430 --> 00:58:55.230
about what's happening around us,

1315
00:58:55.230 --> 00:58:57.780
how we contextualize and make sense of, you know,

1316
00:58:57.780 --> 00:58:59.910
confusing things about our world

1317
00:58:59.910 --> 00:59:01.890
and what it means to be human,

1318
00:59:01.890 --> 00:59:03.843
and in a confusing time,

1319
00:59:04.680 --> 00:59:07.020
I suppose you can make the argument that the artist

1320
00:59:07.020 --> 00:59:10.440
plays a role in trying to create clarity.

1321
00:59:10.440 --> 00:59:12.300
I hope so, you know,

1322
00:59:12.300 --> 00:59:14.160
but clarity for who, right?
Right.

1323
00:59:14.160 --> 00:59:15.360
So, I mean-
Well, usually,

1324
00:59:15.360 --> 00:59:16.800
through their personal experience.

1325
00:59:16.800 --> 00:59:18.180
Exactly, yeah.
Yeah.

1326
00:59:18.180 --> 00:59:21.360
And so, there's an amazing Barbara Kruger show

1327
00:59:21.360 --> 00:59:22.560
up right now in LA,

1328
00:59:22.560 --> 00:59:25.630
and, you know, she's interested

1329
00:59:27.771 --> 00:59:29.910
in the dynamics of power, right?

1330
00:59:29.910 --> 00:59:32.730
And that's something that I'm interested in

1331
00:59:32.730 --> 00:59:36.060
and that I've changed my perspective on, you know,

1332
00:59:36.060 --> 00:59:39.030
certainly as I've grown up, and, you know,

1333
00:59:39.030 --> 00:59:42.123
what is the responsibility of power, and who has it,

1334
00:59:43.907 --> 00:59:47.970
and, yeah, I think art can shed the light on that.

1335
00:59:47.970 --> 00:59:51.450
You know, I'm less interested in art that is didactic,

1336
00:59:51.450 --> 00:59:53.400
just like I'm less interested in people

1337
00:59:53.400 --> 00:59:56.160
who are, you know, prescriptive or didactic.

1338
00:59:56.160 --> 01:00:00.213
You know, I'm interested in openness and expansiveness,

1339
01:00:01.510 --> 01:00:03.750
and I'm more interested in questions

1340
01:00:03.750 --> 01:00:06.480
than I am in answers.
Yeah, Kruger's interesting

1341
01:00:06.480 --> 01:00:08.610
because she's been doing this for a very long time

1342
01:00:08.610 --> 01:00:11.190
and has been very consistent in her messaging,

1343
01:00:11.190 --> 01:00:15.930
and yet has there been a time where her message,

1344
01:00:15.930 --> 01:00:18.570
you know, could be more resonant than it is now?

1345
01:00:18.570 --> 01:00:21.810
Like, I just saw, when I went down to see Rickey,

1346
01:00:21.810 --> 01:00:25.680
the side of the MOCA is like painted in a giant, you know,

1347
01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:27.810
giant, what do you call that?

1348
01:00:27.810 --> 01:00:29.440
It's like a billboard.
Mural kind of, yeah, yeah.

1349
01:00:29.440 --> 01:00:31.440
Like, the whole side of the building is painted

1350
01:00:31.440 --> 01:00:33.300
with one of her pieces.
Yeah.

1351
01:00:33.300 --> 01:00:34.293
So, it's cool.

1352
01:00:35.490 --> 01:00:37.140
We have to keep learning the same things

1353
01:00:37.140 --> 01:00:39.180
over and over again.
(laughs) I know, right?

1354
01:00:39.180 --> 01:00:42.150
Yeah, not just personally, but also as a society,

1355
01:00:42.150 --> 01:00:46.020
you know, and it's like, that can be depressing

1356
01:00:46.020 --> 01:00:48.750
or it can just be a truth, you know?

1357
01:00:48.750 --> 01:00:51.607
I said to my son the other day, you know, I mean,

1358
01:00:51.607 --> 01:00:55.620
"I don't think life is like really circular anymore,

1359
01:00:55.620 --> 01:00:58.860
I'm thinking about it like as a corkscrew," you know?

1360
01:00:58.860 --> 01:01:01.200
So, it's like, yes, you keep coming back around,

1361
01:01:01.200 --> 01:01:02.670
one keeps coming back around,

1362
01:01:02.670 --> 01:01:05.317
and hopefully, you're in a slightly elevated place,

1363
01:01:05.317 --> 01:01:07.830
you know, to experience the same thing,

1364
01:01:07.830 --> 01:01:11.133
so you're not the same as you experience the same thing.

1365
01:01:12.300 --> 01:01:13.893
That's almost like a weird,

1366
01:01:15.480 --> 01:01:17.560
like, kind of "Inception" way of thinking.

1367
01:01:17.560 --> 01:01:19.380
Well, like a metaverse,

1368
01:01:19.380 --> 01:01:23.310
sort of a parallel universe's way of thinking about life

1369
01:01:23.310 --> 01:01:25.650
and sliding doors.
Yeah.

1370
01:01:25.650 --> 01:01:27.360
Well, I mean,

1371
01:01:27.360 --> 01:01:28.740
I catch myself

1372
01:01:28.740 --> 01:01:31.890
in some of the same situations,

1373
01:01:31.890 --> 01:01:35.110
and it's like the knowledge I've accumulated

1374
01:01:36.510 --> 01:01:38.943
allows me to make different decisions.

1375
01:01:40.170 --> 01:01:42.750
Meditation has played a big role in this.

1376
01:01:42.750 --> 01:01:45.030
Yes.
You're on, like, a tear.

1377
01:01:45.030 --> 01:01:47.370
How many days without missing a day?

1378
01:01:47.370 --> 01:01:49.950
It's like 1300 days, or some crazy number like that,

1379
01:01:49.950 --> 01:01:52.260
of consistently meditating.

1380
01:01:52.260 --> 01:01:55.890
So, what has been your experience

1381
01:01:55.890 --> 01:01:59.160
of being so consistent with that practice?

1382
01:01:59.160 --> 01:02:00.780
I'm a totally different person.

1383
01:02:00.780 --> 01:02:04.710
I am, like, certain that my brain has been, like, rewired

1384
01:02:04.710 --> 01:02:09.710
and, like, the space between stimulus and response, right,

1385
01:02:10.020 --> 01:02:11.370
it's much longer.

1386
01:02:11.370 --> 01:02:14.010
So, not every single time, you know?

1387
01:02:14.010 --> 01:02:19.010
There are moments when I still react,

1388
01:02:19.050 --> 01:02:22.110
and most of the time, I respond,

1389
01:02:22.110 --> 01:02:24.420
and that's because of meditation.

1390
01:02:24.420 --> 01:02:25.256
Yeah.

1391
01:02:25.256 --> 01:02:26.423
Anything else?

1392
01:02:27.727 --> 01:02:30.660
You know, I'm a super ritualistic person.

1393
01:02:30.660 --> 01:02:34.050
I do the same things, same five things, every day,

1394
01:02:34.050 --> 01:02:36.033
and I eat the same stuff,

1395
01:02:37.592 --> 01:02:39.180
and actually, apparently,

1396
01:02:39.180 --> 01:02:41.280
I need a little more variety. (laughs)

1397
01:02:41.280 --> 01:02:42.113
I don't know,

1398
01:02:42.113 --> 01:02:44.370
if it's working for you-
I know.

1399
01:02:44.370 --> 01:02:46.410
I'm going back and forth about it, you know?

1400
01:02:46.410 --> 01:02:49.890
Like, my naturopath said I need more variety,

1401
01:02:49.890 --> 01:02:53.190
but my homeopath said, like, it's good, you know,

1402
01:02:53.190 --> 01:02:55.080
so I don't know, who do you listen to?

1403
01:02:55.080 --> 01:02:59.730
I mean, I guess you listen to yourself, but I'm doing this,

1404
01:02:59.730 --> 01:03:03.337
I like, I like to do things that are hard, right?

1405
01:03:03.337 --> 01:03:05.820
You know, we talked about Rickey's project,

1406
01:03:05.820 --> 01:03:09.330
and so one of the things I'm doing in 2022

1407
01:03:09.330 --> 01:03:12.330
is 365 days of yoga,

1408
01:03:12.330 --> 01:03:16.680
and so it's, I'm less about, like, how long I meditate

1409
01:03:16.680 --> 01:03:20.310
or, you know, how long I do yoga for or whatever,

1410
01:03:20.310 --> 01:03:22.020
but it's about the consistency.

1411
01:03:22.020 --> 01:03:24.390
So, I do these same things every day,

1412
01:03:24.390 --> 01:03:28.350
and somehow there's a lot of comfort for me in knowing,

1413
01:03:28.350 --> 01:03:29.943
like, these are just my things.

1414
01:03:33.210 --> 01:03:36.270
It's interesting, in your "Conversations with Artists",

1415
01:03:36.270 --> 01:03:37.590
that ritual comes up,

1416
01:03:37.590 --> 01:03:39.690
like you ask people about their habits.

1417
01:03:39.690 --> 01:03:42.270
Yeah.
And I'm just remembering

1418
01:03:42.270 --> 01:03:43.360
your conversation

1419
01:03:45.120 --> 01:03:48.840
with Tom Sachs and him sharing his morning routine,

1420
01:03:48.840 --> 01:03:51.540
and that really, like, hit me hard.

1421
01:03:51.540 --> 01:03:53.610
Do you remember what he said?

1422
01:03:53.610 --> 01:03:54.750
You know, I don't.

1423
01:03:54.750 --> 01:03:56.850
I remember what he said about, like,

1424
01:03:56.850 --> 01:03:59.340
how he knows what kind of artist someone is

1425
01:03:59.340 --> 01:04:00.672
based on what they're wearing.
Oh, yeah,

1426
01:04:00.672 --> 01:04:03.870
that's a very Werner Herzog thing, or he's like,

1427
01:04:03.870 --> 01:04:05.790
he gives them a broom and says, "Sweep the floor,"

1428
01:04:05.790 --> 01:04:07.380
and he watches how they sweep the floor,

1429
01:04:07.380 --> 01:04:09.330
and then he knows whether they're gonna be a fit.

1430
01:04:09.330 --> 01:04:11.850
Yeah, yeah, but I don't remember what he said,

1431
01:04:11.850 --> 01:04:14.220
I think something about his coffee.

1432
01:04:14.220 --> 01:04:17.820
So, well, he was talking about how we need

1433
01:04:17.820 --> 01:04:20.850
to be very mindful of not squandering those morning hours

1434
01:04:20.850 --> 01:04:22.710
because sleep is this precious thing,

1435
01:04:22.710 --> 01:04:25.350
where your unconscious mind is coming up

1436
01:04:25.350 --> 01:04:27.000
with all of these ideas and, like,

1437
01:04:27.000 --> 01:04:29.370
processing all of this information,

1438
01:04:29.370 --> 01:04:31.560
and when you first wake up in the morning,

1439
01:04:31.560 --> 01:04:33.540
it's accessible and present,

1440
01:04:33.540 --> 01:04:36.240
and to do anything but respect that

1441
01:04:36.240 --> 01:04:38.910
and channel it into something creative

1442
01:04:38.910 --> 01:04:43.050
is to kind of waste that gift that you get every single day.

1443
01:04:43.050 --> 01:04:45.757
And so, he had a couple of sayings, one of which was like,

1444
01:04:45.757 --> 01:04:49.260
"Don't read the newspaper until your hand has touched clay,"

1445
01:04:49.260 --> 01:04:50.790
you know, which could be a metaphor

1446
01:04:50.790 --> 01:04:52.563
for many things, obviously,

1447
01:04:54.305 --> 01:04:56.910
but to treat those morning hours as something

1448
01:04:56.910 --> 01:04:59.100
very special and sacred.
Yeah.

1449
01:04:59.100 --> 01:05:03.870
I thank you for that, I do remember that now,

1450
01:05:03.870 --> 01:05:05.910
and I think a lot about that, too,

1451
01:05:05.910 --> 01:05:09.393
and that's a big part of why I have this morning ritual,

1452
01:05:11.673 --> 01:05:13.383
because I do believe that,

1453
01:05:15.750 --> 01:05:17.880
and it's not just me, I mean, this is,

1454
01:05:17.880 --> 01:05:20.070
a lot of people think this and have said this, you know,

1455
01:05:20.070 --> 01:05:22.380
but the way that you start your day, you know,

1456
01:05:22.380 --> 01:05:24.000
sets the tone for your whole day,

1457
01:05:24.000 --> 01:05:26.850
and if you populate your mind with, like,

1458
01:05:26.850 --> 01:05:31.290
other people's thoughts or problems or, you know, whatever,

1459
01:05:31.290 --> 01:05:33.480
it changes your emotion.

1460
01:05:33.480 --> 01:05:36.630
And so, you know, I love the morning,

1461
01:05:36.630 --> 01:05:39.750
and that's my most kind of creative time,

1462
01:05:39.750 --> 01:05:41.133
most productive time,

1463
01:05:42.929 --> 01:05:45.090
and it, yeah, it sets my tone for the day,

1464
01:05:45.090 --> 01:05:49.020
and any day that I even don't meditate until, like,

1465
01:05:49.020 --> 01:05:52.140
later in the day, it just doesn't go as well.

1466
01:05:52.140 --> 01:05:54.690
Yeah, you've got to do it first thing, right?

1467
01:05:54.690 --> 01:05:58.140
Among all of these many, many conversations that you've had

1468
01:05:58.140 --> 01:06:00.093
with artists for many years,

1469
01:06:00.960 --> 01:06:03.330
obviously there's gonna be recurring themes

1470
01:06:03.330 --> 01:06:07.290
or things that kind of connect all of these people

1471
01:06:07.290 --> 01:06:09.240
in some sort of unified theory.

1472
01:06:09.240 --> 01:06:12.000
So, what is that for you?

1473
01:06:12.000 --> 01:06:13.680
What do you glean

1474
01:06:13.680 --> 01:06:18.090
from speaking to all of these highly creative people

1475
01:06:18.090 --> 01:06:19.770
that would be meaningful for somebody

1476
01:06:19.770 --> 01:06:22.170
who's listening to this who maybe doesn't feel

1477
01:06:22.170 --> 01:06:25.263
that connected to art or the creative process?

1478
01:06:26.700 --> 01:06:30.450
I think one of the things that comes up a lot,

1479
01:06:30.450 --> 01:06:33.570
and people use different terms for it,

1480
01:06:33.570 --> 01:06:35.910
but it's the same thing,

1481
01:06:35.910 --> 01:06:39.720
and it's about kind of the flow state

1482
01:06:39.720 --> 01:06:42.270
and, you know, what do you do in your life

1483
01:06:42.270 --> 01:06:46.113
that allows you to forget everything else,

1484
01:06:47.040 --> 01:06:50.940
and, you know, when do you most feel like yourself,

1485
01:06:50.940 --> 01:06:54.690
and it's when you're not aware of what's happening.

1486
01:06:54.690 --> 01:06:57.930
And so, you know, I had a friend of mine,

1487
01:06:57.930 --> 01:07:01.320
who is the CEO of the Aspen Skiing Company, on the podcast,

1488
01:07:01.320 --> 01:07:03.240
and, you know, he finds it on the mountain.

1489
01:07:03.240 --> 01:07:05.643
You know, he finds it in skiing, right?

1490
01:07:06.612 --> 01:07:09.030
And so, everyone does something

1491
01:07:09.030 --> 01:07:10.707
when they feel that in the day.

1492
01:07:10.707 --> 01:07:12.960
For some people, it's washing the dishes, you know.

1493
01:07:12.960 --> 01:07:15.000
It's like the warm water on your hands, you know.

1494
01:07:15.000 --> 01:07:17.290
Some people, it's, you know, running

1495
01:07:19.677 --> 01:07:21.210
or, you know, walking the dog.

1496
01:07:21.210 --> 01:07:22.380
You know, for most people,

1497
01:07:22.380 --> 01:07:25.590
it is like somehow being in motion

1498
01:07:25.590 --> 01:07:29.580
and/or being removed from your devices, you know.

1499
01:07:29.580 --> 01:07:31.880
So, for me, it's also in the shower, you know.

1500
01:07:33.030 --> 01:07:37.920
I think that in that space, like, that's where art happens,

1501
01:07:37.920 --> 01:07:39.577
and it's like the art of life as well.

1502
01:07:39.577 --> 01:07:42.870
You know, that's when you solve a problem

1503
01:07:42.870 --> 01:07:44.970
that, you know, has been plaguing you,

1504
01:07:44.970 --> 01:07:47.167
even, you know, whatever it is.

1505
01:07:47.167 --> 01:07:49.620
You know, it might be something that's technical

1506
01:07:49.620 --> 01:07:52.050
or mechanical, or it might be something that's, you know,

1507
01:07:52.050 --> 01:07:54.240
conceptual or emotional,

1508
01:07:54.240 --> 01:07:57.000
but being able to tap into that space,

1509
01:07:57.000 --> 01:07:59.223
this is something that everyone talks about.

1510
01:08:00.120 --> 01:08:01.680
That's the mystical side of it,

1511
01:08:01.680 --> 01:08:04.350
but I think there's also a very practical side of it.

1512
01:08:04.350 --> 01:08:08.250
I had Van Neistat in here, who worked with Tom,

1513
01:08:08.250 --> 01:08:11.610
back to Tom Sachs, worked with Tom for a long time,

1514
01:08:11.610 --> 01:08:14.120
and that episode was great 'cause he, like, disabuses you

1515
01:08:14.120 --> 01:08:18.090
of any idea that, you know, the artist is walking around,

1516
01:08:18.090 --> 01:08:20.970
just having lightning bulb moments constantly

1517
01:08:20.970 --> 01:08:23.790
and just, you know, sort of expressing,

1518
01:08:23.790 --> 01:08:26.250
you know, without any kind of toil or turmoil,

1519
01:08:26.250 --> 01:08:28.230
and that, in truth,

1520
01:08:28.230 --> 01:08:32.730
it's the result of a very diligent commitment to a practice

1521
01:08:32.730 --> 01:08:36.570
that is organized and well thought out,

1522
01:08:36.570 --> 01:08:40.320
and often extremely difficult and time-consuming.

1523
01:08:40.320 --> 01:08:41.730
All those things are true,

1524
01:08:41.730 --> 01:08:44.340
and what I would add to that as well

1525
01:08:44.340 --> 01:08:46.950
is that it's often lonely, you know?

1526
01:08:46.950 --> 01:08:51.210
And I think that loneliness is something that people

1527
01:08:51.210 --> 01:08:55.530
don't talk about a lot, and I think that it is like

1528
01:08:55.530 --> 01:08:59.850
a super broad, societal problem, you know?

1529
01:08:59.850 --> 01:09:03.900
And there's a benefit to loneliness, or alone time,

1530
01:09:03.900 --> 01:09:06.342
and it's not necessarily the same.

1531
01:09:06.342 --> 01:09:08.970
You know, you can be alone and not lonely,

1532
01:09:08.970 --> 01:09:13.650
and I think loneliness is more prevalent than people allow

1533
01:09:13.650 --> 01:09:17.280
because it seems like, you know, like maudlin

1534
01:09:17.280 --> 01:09:19.890
or, you know, like self, you know,

1535
01:09:19.890 --> 01:09:22.113
negative in some way, you know,

1536
01:09:23.550 --> 01:09:28.360
but that idea of being alone and/or being lonely

1537
01:09:29.280 --> 01:09:30.720
is necessary

1538
01:09:30.720 --> 01:09:32.800
for creativity as well

1539
01:09:34.454 --> 01:09:36.240
and not something that comes up, too.

1540
01:09:36.240 --> 01:09:38.910
Right, well, that plays into a bit of a stereotype,

1541
01:09:38.910 --> 01:09:42.120
that to be a brilliant, successful artist,

1542
01:09:42.120 --> 01:09:43.780
you must be tortured

1543
01:09:44.745 --> 01:09:48.240
and alone and isolated and self-flagellating,

1544
01:09:48.240 --> 01:09:51.870
and all of these things, but you know lots of artists.

1545
01:09:51.870 --> 01:09:55.620
Can you draw a conclusion about the general disposition

1546
01:09:55.620 --> 01:09:58.380
of the artist, or do they just come in all shapes

1547
01:09:58.380 --> 01:10:00.000
and sizes and flavors?
Artists are people,

1548
01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:03.000
you know, and all those adjectives that you just used,

1549
01:10:03.000 --> 01:10:05.238
like, they can all be teased out, you know?

1550
01:10:05.238 --> 01:10:06.405
So, it's like,

1551
01:10:07.980 --> 01:10:09.273
you can be,

1552
01:10:10.560 --> 01:10:14.400
you know, heady and contemplative

1553
01:10:14.400 --> 01:10:15.870
without being tortured, right?

1554
01:10:15.870 --> 01:10:16.893
You can be,

1555
01:10:18.270 --> 01:10:22.050
you know, tortured, but still, like, into it, you know,

1556
01:10:22.050 --> 01:10:23.973
like maybe that's not negative, right?

1557
01:10:25.380 --> 01:10:26.280
I mean,

1558
01:10:26.280 --> 01:10:30.120
I think that, you know, artists are people

1559
01:10:30.120 --> 01:10:35.120
who make things for other people, you know, and this idea

1560
01:10:35.280 --> 01:10:37.420
of, like, the mythology

1561
01:10:38.545 --> 01:10:42.303
of any kind of, like, group is almost always wrong.

1562
01:10:43.890 --> 01:10:47.220
Yeah, that idea of artists make things for other people

1563
01:10:47.220 --> 01:10:49.170
is an interesting one and, I feel,

1564
01:10:49.170 --> 01:10:50.700
like very dynamic in our age.

1565
01:10:50.700 --> 01:10:52.050
We were talking about Daniel Arsham

1566
01:10:52.050 --> 01:10:55.350
before the podcast and how his work

1567
01:10:55.350 --> 01:10:59.190
is one of (clears throat) constant collaboration with, like,

1568
01:10:59.190 --> 01:11:02.670
corporate partners, some of which are super cool,

1569
01:11:02.670 --> 01:11:04.110
like, oh, Adidas, and stuff like that,

1570
01:11:04.110 --> 01:11:06.330
and some that are like,

1571
01:11:06.330 --> 01:11:08.670
really, you're gonna work with that brand?

1572
01:11:08.670 --> 01:11:11.670
But in so doing, he's able to create

1573
01:11:11.670 --> 01:11:14.160
these sort of iconic works

1574
01:11:14.160 --> 01:11:16.620
that live outside of the museum space

1575
01:11:16.620 --> 01:11:18.900
and kind of penetrate culture

1576
01:11:18.900 --> 01:11:20.760
in a really interesting way.

1577
01:11:20.760 --> 01:11:23.760
It feels like that, that kind of sensibility,

1578
01:11:23.760 --> 01:11:26.040
maybe began with somebody like Warhol,

1579
01:11:26.040 --> 01:11:27.600
and he's carrying on that tradition,

1580
01:11:27.600 --> 01:11:32.010
but now, in this hyper-corporatized social media world,

1581
01:11:32.010 --> 01:11:35.340
us being Gen Xers and having hangups about selling out

1582
01:11:35.340 --> 01:11:37.230
is, like, not a thing anymore.

1583
01:11:37.230 --> 01:11:38.460
You know, it's like, oh, cool,

1584
01:11:38.460 --> 01:11:40.590
you're working with that brand,

1585
01:11:40.590 --> 01:11:44.550
or you're taking money from these people to make that thing.

1586
01:11:44.550 --> 01:11:47.100
We have a very different relationship, too,

1587
01:11:47.100 --> 01:11:49.860
than when we were in our 20s.
Yeah.

1588
01:11:49.860 --> 01:11:53.160
You know, I think I spend a lot of time

1589
01:11:53.160 --> 01:11:54.840
thinking about two things.

1590
01:11:54.840 --> 01:11:57.360
One, I really try and ask myself,

1591
01:11:57.360 --> 01:11:59.550
like, what would I love, you know,

1592
01:11:59.550 --> 01:12:02.100
and it goes back to the story that I told at the beginning

1593
01:12:02.100 --> 01:12:03.903
about Gary, you know, my stepfather.

1594
01:12:04.830 --> 01:12:06.300
You know, not what would I like,

1595
01:12:06.300 --> 01:12:08.280
you know, not like who do I love,

1596
01:12:08.280 --> 01:12:10.110
you know, but like what would I love,

1597
01:12:10.110 --> 01:12:13.770
you know, that allowance of the thing that would be,

1598
01:12:13.770 --> 01:12:15.450
you know, the greatest, highest,

1599
01:12:15.450 --> 01:12:17.460
best use, et cetera, you know?

1600
01:12:17.460 --> 01:12:20.733
And then, the other thing I think a lot about is,

1601
01:12:22.320 --> 01:12:24.960
you know, why does it matter,

1602
01:12:24.960 --> 01:12:27.570
you know, or what matters most, or who cares?

1603
01:12:27.570 --> 01:12:29.880
You know, it's kind of like the same question,

1604
01:12:29.880 --> 01:12:33.180
and being able to answer that, and one of the things

1605
01:12:33.180 --> 01:12:36.540
that matters a lot to me is amplification.

1606
01:12:36.540 --> 01:12:38.730
You know, it's like, if you're gonna do something,

1607
01:12:38.730 --> 01:12:41.700
like, you gotta make it worth doing,

1608
01:12:41.700 --> 01:12:44.370
and part of that is about its impact.

1609
01:12:44.370 --> 01:12:48.690
And so, you know, brand collaborations or things that, yeah,

1610
01:12:48.690 --> 01:12:50.707
previously, maybe I would've said, like,

1611
01:12:50.707 --> 01:12:52.980
"Well, I wouldn't do that," or like, you know, "No way,"

1612
01:12:52.980 --> 01:12:55.677
I'm like, "Okay, that's a vehicle," you know?

1613
01:12:55.677 --> 01:12:58.650
So, like Malcolm McLaren, you know, what's the message,

1614
01:12:58.650 --> 01:13:00.840
what's the medium, right?
Right, right, right.

1615
01:13:00.840 --> 01:13:02.485
And being okay about that.
Right.

1616
01:13:02.485 --> 01:13:04.803
And actually being grateful for it.

1617
01:13:06.540 --> 01:13:08.280
Yeah, I mean, when you,

1618
01:13:08.280 --> 01:13:10.050
I'm just remembering, like, around the time

1619
01:13:10.050 --> 01:13:12.450
when we were living in New York, everywhere you would go,

1620
01:13:12.450 --> 01:13:15.330
you would see Ravs painted on the side of buildings,

1621
01:13:15.330 --> 01:13:17.977
or you would see André the Giant, and you'd be like,

1622
01:13:17.977 --> 01:13:19.950
"Why is André the Giant everywhere,"

1623
01:13:19.950 --> 01:13:22.980
but there's something cool and provocative about that.

1624
01:13:22.980 --> 01:13:26.310
There's a mystery, like what is behind this,

1625
01:13:26.310 --> 01:13:28.380
like why are there pictures of this person

1626
01:13:28.380 --> 01:13:30.900
all over the city, and who's doing this,

1627
01:13:30.900 --> 01:13:33.750
that I think is really interesting in that sensibility,

1628
01:13:33.750 --> 01:13:36.657
like the urban landscape is the museum.

1629
01:13:36.657 --> 01:13:39.280
And I think that's why street art

1630
01:13:39.280 --> 01:13:41.040
is so kind of compelling and interesting,

1631
01:13:41.040 --> 01:13:44.370
because it is solving that accessibility problem,

1632
01:13:44.370 --> 01:13:48.783
and it is obviously democratizing the experience of art,

1633
01:13:51.420 --> 01:13:55.120
and it feels like it's this merging

1634
01:13:56.280 --> 01:13:58.320
of kind of culture

1635
01:13:58.320 --> 01:14:01.350
with art in the most seamless way possible, right?

1636
01:14:01.350 --> 01:14:02.610
Yeah.
So, how do you take

1637
01:14:02.610 --> 01:14:04.353
the best of what that is,

1638
01:14:05.250 --> 01:14:07.410
with you being somebody who's in charge of, like,

1639
01:14:07.410 --> 01:14:10.470
a building, you know, and encapsulate that in a way

1640
01:14:10.470 --> 01:14:13.230
that is meaningful for somebody who's coming in?

1641
01:14:13.230 --> 01:14:17.970
Yeah, and what I would also share in response to that

1642
01:14:17.970 --> 01:14:21.930
is like, Claude Monet's "Water Lilies", right?

1643
01:14:21.930 --> 01:14:24.510
I mean, I had a time, like in my 20s,

1644
01:14:24.510 --> 01:14:27.360
where I'm like, oh, God, you know, if it's on a dish towel,

1645
01:14:27.360 --> 01:14:30.060
like, I'm never looking at that art again, right,

1646
01:14:30.060 --> 01:14:32.550
and then going,

1647
01:14:32.550 --> 01:14:36.000
and on Naoshima Island, seeing these, you know,

1648
01:14:36.000 --> 01:14:40.500
incredible Claude Monet "Water Lilies" with natural light

1649
01:14:40.500 --> 01:14:43.020
and remembering like, oh, the reason that this

1650
01:14:43.020 --> 01:14:45.570
like jumped the shark, you know, and became part

1651
01:14:45.570 --> 01:14:48.780
of popular culture is 'cause it's amazing,

1652
01:14:48.780 --> 01:14:50.610
you know, because it's beautiful,

1653
01:14:50.610 --> 01:14:54.240
and I like things that are beautiful.

1654
01:14:54.240 --> 01:14:56.430
I wanted to read from your book.

1655
01:14:56.430 --> 01:14:59.980
You had mentioned earlier about the idea

1656
01:15:01.598 --> 01:15:02.790
of what would you love,

1657
01:15:02.790 --> 01:15:06.967
and you say in the latest book, in this volume,

1658
01:15:06.967 --> 01:15:09.780
"So, what if we ask ourselves in every moment of every hour

1659
01:15:09.780 --> 01:15:14.010
of every day for the rest of our lives, "What would I love?"

1660
01:15:14.010 --> 01:15:16.410
What if we allow ourselves the incredible gift

1661
01:15:16.410 --> 01:15:19.830
of connecting with our truest and deepest desires?

1662
01:15:19.830 --> 01:15:22.350
What if we believe we are not just able

1663
01:15:22.350 --> 01:15:24.870
or entitled to do so, but that we also know

1664
01:15:24.870 --> 01:15:28.020
that by granting ourselves this gift of connection,

1665
01:15:28.020 --> 01:15:30.753
we make the world better, better for ourselves,

1666
01:15:31.680 --> 01:15:34.710
our partners, our children, the planet?

1667
01:15:34.710 --> 01:15:36.870
What if the space of art encourages

1668
01:15:36.870 --> 01:15:39.000
and reinforces this notion?

1669
01:15:39.000 --> 01:15:40.680
What if the artist, by living fully

1670
01:15:40.680 --> 01:15:43.560
and outwardly and inspiringly and generously,

1671
01:15:43.560 --> 01:15:47.010
shares this notion as a woman, a mother, a partner,

1672
01:15:47.010 --> 01:15:51.480
a yogi, a generator, a friend, a community member?

1673
01:15:51.480 --> 01:15:54.120
What if paintings and sculptures and installations

1674
01:15:54.120 --> 01:15:58.560
can become incantations, prayers, experiences?

1675
01:15:58.560 --> 01:16:00.750
What if all the things are not just what they are,

1676
01:16:00.750 --> 01:16:02.670
but also more?

1677
01:16:02.670 --> 01:16:04.240
What if the entire universe

1678
01:16:05.820 --> 01:16:09.510
can be not only captured, but also encompassed,

1679
01:16:09.510 --> 01:16:13.980
both in the work of art and in the experience of it?

1680
01:16:13.980 --> 01:16:16.740
This is not just what I believe, but also what I know.

1681
01:16:16.740 --> 01:16:18.900
This is how I am inspired.

1682
01:16:18.900 --> 01:16:20.130
This is how I practice.

1683
01:16:20.130 --> 01:16:21.570
This is how I learn.

1684
01:16:21.570 --> 01:16:23.400
This is how I love.

1685
01:16:23.400 --> 01:16:25.140
This is how I am.

1686
01:16:25.140 --> 01:16:26.310
Amen."

1687
01:16:26.310 --> 01:16:29.010
So, it is a prayer in and of itself.

1688
01:16:29.010 --> 01:16:29.943
It's beautiful.

1689
01:16:33.369 --> 01:16:35.250
I haven't had that read to me.

1690
01:16:35.250 --> 01:16:36.443
Thank you.
Yeah.

1691
01:16:37.280 --> 01:16:40.980
How did you come to that approach

1692
01:16:40.980 --> 01:16:42.333
to how you think about art?

1693
01:16:43.500 --> 01:16:46.953
You know, in listening to you read that, I'm like,

1694
01:16:49.320 --> 01:16:52.650
how audacious of me to say that. (laughs)

1695
01:16:52.650 --> 01:16:56.850
Yeah, but you're entitled to own that, you know?

1696
01:16:56.850 --> 01:16:59.280
Yeah, how did I get there?

1697
01:16:59.280 --> 01:17:00.113
You know,

1698
01:17:02.539 --> 01:17:06.810
there are so many answers that I could give,

1699
01:17:06.810 --> 01:17:10.440
and it's sort of like the question of, you know,

1700
01:17:10.440 --> 01:17:12.213
how did I find my way to art?

1701
01:17:16.530 --> 01:17:18.990
I trace kind of,

1702
01:17:18.990 --> 01:17:22.890
I don't know if it's Heidi 2.0 or 3.0 or 6.0,

1703
01:17:22.890 --> 01:17:26.490
or whatever it is, but whoever I am, like, right now,

1704
01:17:26.490 --> 01:17:28.177
I trace a lot of it back

1705
01:17:30.155 --> 01:17:34.050
to the opportunity to meet this guy, Rand Stagen,

1706
01:17:34.050 --> 01:17:36.850
who has something called the Stagen Institute in Dallas,

1707
01:17:38.550 --> 01:17:40.380
and his introduction of me

1708
01:17:40.380 --> 01:17:43.530
to Joseph Campbell's "The Hero's Journey"

1709
01:17:43.530 --> 01:17:46.890
and giving me a framework for understanding

1710
01:17:46.890 --> 01:17:49.740
my life up until that moment,

1711
01:17:49.740 --> 01:17:52.110
and then marking that time

1712
01:17:52.110 --> 01:17:57.110
with a public talk, you know, for 300 people, all YPOers,

1713
01:17:57.210 --> 01:18:01.500
and then having him, you know, hug me at the end of it

1714
01:18:01.500 --> 01:18:04.110
and say, "You know, you just made a declaration

1715
01:18:04.110 --> 01:18:05.610
in front of your community,

1716
01:18:05.610 --> 01:18:07.920
and I'm so excited to see what happens."

1717
01:18:07.920 --> 01:18:09.680
And I was like, "What's a declaration,"

1718
01:18:09.680 --> 01:18:11.813
(laughing) you know, like what do you mean,

1719
01:18:14.572 --> 01:18:19.521
and it's a big part of that, you know, this idea

1720
01:18:19.521 --> 01:18:23.490
that there is some kind of system or structure

1721
01:18:23.490 --> 01:18:25.210
by which to quantify

1722
01:18:27.450 --> 01:18:29.433
and allow things that,

1723
01:18:30.330 --> 01:18:33.630
at the time, seemed like terrible, you know,

1724
01:18:33.630 --> 01:18:37.500
or difficult or awful or embarrassing or shameful,

1725
01:18:37.500 --> 01:18:41.790
and to see them within this space

1726
01:18:41.790 --> 01:18:43.770
of gratitude, you know,

1727
01:18:43.770 --> 01:18:46.320
for every single thing that's happened.

1728
01:18:46.320 --> 01:18:47.850
Right, 'cause they all hold meaning

1729
01:18:47.850 --> 01:18:50.446
that led you to this place.
Yeah.

1730
01:18:50.446 --> 01:18:51.690
Right?
Yeah,

1731
01:18:51.690 --> 01:18:56.040
and you know, for me, art is my North Star,

1732
01:18:56.040 --> 01:18:56.940
you know?

1733
01:18:56.940 --> 01:18:58.140
I love nature,

1734
01:18:58.140 --> 01:19:00.430
and that is a place

1735
01:19:02.565 --> 01:19:05.400
of, like, centering and grounding for me, you know,

1736
01:19:05.400 --> 01:19:09.490
and, like, the consistent thing through my life

1737
01:19:11.040 --> 01:19:13.990
for inspiration and, you know, joy

1738
01:19:15.137 --> 01:19:16.590
and company

1739
01:19:16.590 --> 01:19:17.423
is art.

1740
01:19:18.600 --> 01:19:21.330
You obviously have a really good radar

1741
01:19:21.330 --> 01:19:25.800
for people who are doing something interesting

1742
01:19:25.800 --> 01:19:27.180
and seeking out these artists.

1743
01:19:27.180 --> 01:19:28.560
So, you know, what is that?

1744
01:19:28.560 --> 01:19:31.530
Like, what are you looking for, or what is it

1745
01:19:31.530 --> 01:19:35.220
that triggers in you, like, oh, that person's interesting,

1746
01:19:35.220 --> 01:19:37.170
I want to know more about who they are?

1747
01:19:38.070 --> 01:19:41.373
It's often something that I don't understand.

1748
01:19:43.470 --> 01:19:45.420
We had an interesting conversation.

1749
01:19:45.420 --> 01:19:48.690
So, we spent Thanksgiving in Copenhagen with Laura

1750
01:19:48.690 --> 01:19:51.720
